00:05:56.149 --> 00:05:58.250
There  was  a  major  pushback,
both  from  multinational

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:01.350
corporations,  but  also  governance,
that  lobbied  heavily  against

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.449
data  protection  and  privacy
rules  in  the  European  Union.

00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:07.670
But  the  public  outcry  and  the
global  dimension  of  the  scandal  that

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:11.470
Edward  Snowden  helped  disclose  helped
fight  that  in  a  really  crucial  way.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.110
And  although  the  GDPR  may  not
be  perfect  in  many  ways,  it

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.990
has  become  a  gold  standard  for
data  protection  regulation  and

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.250
it  has  inspired  a  range  of
different  laws  around  the  globe.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:24.829
And  yeah,  the  Snowden  revelations
also  had  a  second  really

00:06:24.829 --> 00:06:27.569
important  impact,  which  was
that  there  was  sort  of  like  a

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:31.589
global  reckoning  with  privacy
from  a  consumer  perspective.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.810
So  many  people  simply  expected
their  privacy  and  their

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:38.149
data  to  be  protected,  which  led
to  a  real  spike  in  consumer

00:06:38.149 --> 00:06:41.649
demand  for  secure  communication
services  and  devices.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:45.810
And  some  really  popular  apps,
like  WhatsApp  or  Viber,  if  anyone

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:49.629
remembers,  started  launching
internet  encrypted  services,  which

00:06:49.629 --> 00:06:52.470
others  then  tried  to  emulate,
like  Telegram,  not  so  secure.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:56.029
But  we  did  see  a  general
proliferation  of  internet

00:06:56.029 --> 00:06:59.550
encryption  and  yeah,  that
was  a  real  big  change.

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:03.230
But  of  course,  the  2010s  also  had
a  really  dark  side  and  there  was  a

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.769
pushback  to  this  proliferation  of
internet  encryption  from  law  enforcement.

00:07:07.149 --> 00:07:11.029
And  one  really  crucial  actor
in  this  fight  was  this  lady,

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:15.110
Valerie  Caproni,  who  was  then
the  general  counsel  of  the  FBI.

00:07:15.529 --> 00:07:20.769
And  she  was  the  first  one  to  describe
this  ongoing  proliferation  of  internet

00:07:20.769 --> 00:07:26.050
encrypted  communication  as  going  dark,  which
has  become  a  major  theme  in  this  fight.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:30.410
So  going  dark  is  the  idea  that  because
of  the  spread  of  internet  encryption,  law

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:35.189
enforcement  loses  access  to  data  law
enforcement  needs  in  order  to  do  its  job.

00:07:35.189 --> 00:07:39.250
And  this  framing  of  going  dark  kind
of  applies  that  law  enforcement  has  a

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.850
natural  right  to  that  data,  which  is
now  being  upended  by  internet  encryption.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.589
And  this  sounds  silly  to  us,
but  this  idea  of  going  dark  has

00:07:47.589 --> 00:07:51.370
been  one  of  the  most  successful
framings  ever  deployed  by  law

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.850
enforcement  globally  in  trying
to  attain  more  access  to  data.

00:07:55.129 --> 00:07:58.370
Which  is,  of  course,  ironic  because  already
in  2010s,  we  were  living  in  a  world

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:03.790
where  more  data  was  being  created,
collected,  and  processed  constantly  and  law

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.550
enforcement  had  more  access  data  than
ever  before  in  the  history  of  mankind.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:10.189
But  still,  we're  now  in  a
new  era  of  the  crypto  wars,

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:13.170
which  is  about  secure
communication  and  devices.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:18.670
Chloé: And  one  of  the  key  kind  of  illustrative
case  of  this  new  crypto  war  is  the

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:24.870
San  Bernardino  case  in  2015,  which  set
the  FBI  and  Apple  against  each  other.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:29.589
And  the  story  is  that  the  FBI  found  an
iPhone  belonging  to  one  of  the  perpetrators

00:08:29.589 --> 00:08:36.470
of  this  mass  shooting.  and  tried  to  compel
Apple  to  assist  them  in  unlocking  it.

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:40.409
Apple  challenged  the  order  in
courts,  but  before  we  got  to

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:45.870
a  decision,  the  FBI  found  an
Australian  hacking  firm  which

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:50.210
found  a  way  to  bypass  the
iPhone  security  protections.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:54.250
This  case  really  sealed
the  going  dark  framing.

00:08:54.950 --> 00:09:03.269
In  2016,  FBI  director  James  Comey
testified  twice  in  the  Congress  reiterating

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:08.429
concerns  that  encryption  was  allowing
criminals  to  go  unpunished  and  that

00:09:08.429 --> 00:09:13.190
it  was  making  it  too  difficult  for
law  enforcement  to  access  evidence.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:22.129
And  so  since  2011,  we  see  regular
push  in  the  public  discourse,  in  the

00:09:22.129 --> 00:09:27.370
media,  from  law  enforcement  and
intelligence  agencies  to  address  this

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:31.009
in  regulation,  in  legislation,  to
address  this  encryption  problem.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:37.389
This  is  an  example,  a  statement
published  in  2020  by  the  Five  Eyes.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.830
The  Five  Eyes  are  composed
of  the  intelligence  agencies

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:46.450
from  Australia,  New  Zealand,
the  UK,  US,  and  Canada.

00:09:46.929 --> 00:09:50.230
And  they  basically  lament
about  [inaudable]  encryption

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.330
and  call  for  measures
to  access  encrypted  data.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:55.809
Svea: And  then  something  major  happened.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.549
The  data  retention  directive
that  Chloé  introduced

00:09:58.549 --> 00:10:00.990
earlier  was  struck  down  by
the  European  Court  of  Justice.

00:10:01.289 --> 00:10:06.389
So  the  court  found  that  actually  general
and  indiscriminate  retention  of  all  traffic

00:10:06.389 --> 00:10:11.289
and  location  data  is  contrary  to  EU  law
and  that  the  goal  of  combating  crime  does

00:10:11.289 --> 00:10:15.309
not  actually  make  it  okay  to  infringe
on  people's  fundamentalist  rights  on  to

00:10:15.309 --> 00:10:18.549
privacy  and  data  protection  as  laid  down
by  the  Charter  of  Fundamental  Rights.

00:10:18.549 --> 00:10:23.750
So  this  meant  that  suddenly  there  was  no  EU
-wide  framework  for  data  retention  anymore.

00:10:23.909 --> 00:10:25.269
And  this  was  a  really  good  day.

00:10:27.269 --> 00:10:29.929
But  of  course,  unfortunately,
there  was  one  major  issue.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:33.789
Bear  with  me  for  a  second.  So  there  are
essentially  two  types  of  European  law.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.370
One  is  a  directive,  which
means  that  member  states  have

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.970
to  transpose  European
regulation  into  a  national  law.

00:10:40.149 --> 00:10:42.230
And  the  other  one  is  a
regulation,  which  means  that  law

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.909
becomes  immediately  applicable
across  the  European  Union.

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:46.190
This  was  a  directive.

00:10:46.509 --> 00:10:49.409
So  essentially  what  happened  was
that  member  states  had  already

00:10:49.409 --> 00:10:52.370
transposed  the  directive  into
national  law  and  just  ignored

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:55.370
the  judgment  of  the  court,  which
is  really  fascinating  to  see.

00:10:56.029 --> 00:10:59.350
And  there  was  then  a  later
study  a  couple  years  later  that

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.909
essentially  showed  that  more  than
50  member  states  didn't  make

00:11:01.909 --> 00:11:05.750
any  significant  changes  after
the  directive  was  struck  down.

00:11:06.129 --> 00:11:10.649
And  a  number  of  member  states,  at
least  three,  have  indicated  that  their

00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:14.710
current  regulations  on  data  retentions
are  based  on  the  directive  that

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:18.549
has  been  found  illegal  and  in
violation  of  European  fundamental  rights.

00:11:18.769 --> 00:11:21.629
So  even  though  we  had  this
major  win  at  court,  data

00:11:21.629 --> 00:11:23.750
retention  continued  to  persist
in  the  European  Union.

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:29.649
Chloé: And  this  is  why  2014  and  the  judgment
by  the  Court  of  Justice  in  the  digital

00:11:29.649 --> 00:11:35.230
rights  RLN  case  was  only  the  beginning
of  the  infamous  data  retention  saga.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:36.370
Why?

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:41.230
Because  people  started  launching
litigation  cases  against  each  national

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.649
legislation  which  had  been  transposing
the  data  retention  directive.

00:11:44.649 --> 00:11:48.970
So  we  had  a  case  in  Sweden,
we  had  a  case  in  France,

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:52.629
UK,  Belgium,  Germany,
and  so  on  and  so  forth.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:58.830
So  it  led  to  a  number  of  judgments
throughout  the  past  10  years.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:03.690
And  so  what  we  have,  the  result  of  this
is  that  we  have  a  very  rich  jurisprudence,

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:08.610
very  detailed,  the  courts  has  to  go
into  so  much  detail,  about  what  is

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.889
possible  to  retain  under  EU  law  and
what  is  not,  and  under  which  conditions.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:19.490
The  problem  also  on  the  other  side,
politically  speaking,  civil  society

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:23.129
has  been  pushing  the  commission  to
take  action  against  those  member

00:12:23.129 --> 00:12:27.490
states  who  refuse  to  respect  the
jurisprudence  of  the  Court  of  Justice.

00:12:27.809 --> 00:12:31.909
We  asked  the  commission,  which  has
this  competence  to  launch  infringement

00:12:31.909 --> 00:12:37.149
procedures  against  member  states  which
derogate  from  EU  law,  so  to  sanction

00:12:37.149 --> 00:12:41.990
them,  and  the  commission  always  pushed
back  and  refused  to  do  so,  although  they

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:46.549
have  the  competence  to,  they  are  like
the  guardian  of  the  treaties,  so  to  say,

00:12:46.649 --> 00:12:50.389
so  they  are  responsible  to  make  sure
that  EU  law  is  applied  by  member  states.

00:12:50.669 --> 00:12:55.490
So  it's  really  a  political  choice  by
the  commission  to  not  act  at  all  for  10

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:59.110
years  and  letting  rogue  member  states
continue  with  their  data  retention  laws.

00:13:00.129 --> 00:13:05.669
This  brings  us  to  the
2010s.  Where  am  I?  Yes.

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:09.470
We  talked  about  the
mainstreaming  and  multiplication

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:13.370
of  rather  popular  encrypted
messaging  applications.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:19.629
There  were  also  a  few  more  lesser -known
