commission  has  decided  to  actually,  or

00:27:10.519 --> 00:27:14.140
they  have  essentially  said  that  this  group
will  be  in  existence  for  a  year,  and

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:18.500
then  after  that,  the  commission
will  deliver  another  roadmap,  a

00:27:19.779 --> 00:27:23.380
technical  roadmap  on  encryption.  So  the
commission  has  essentially  indicated  that

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:27.759
there  will  be  some  sort  of  report,  some
sort  of  final  output  from  that  group  in  a

00:27:27.759 --> 00:27:32.140
year  from  now  or  so.  Interestingly
enough,  it's  still  really  unclear  what

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:37.559
happens  afterwards.  So  essentially  the
commission,  by  convening  these  expert

00:27:37.559 --> 00:27:41.059
groups,  tries  to  depoliticize  an  issue,
right?  They  essentially  take  this

00:27:41.059 --> 00:27:46.119
highly  political  question,  which  is,  should
we  as  democracies  figure  out  ways  to

00:27:46.119 --> 00:27:49.859
build  in  front  doors  to  access
encrypted  communication  and  give  that  to

00:27:49.859 --> 00:27:52.819
an  expert  group,  which  was
of  course  not  democratically

00:27:52.819 --> 00:27:56.500
elected,  but  then  any  of  the  day,
of  course,  the  commission  retains  the

00:27:56.500 --> 00:28:01.039
right  to  take  a  political  decision  on
whether  to  legislate  on  this  issue  or  not.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:05.099
So  we're  sort  of  see  this  like  this
technocratic  washing  machine  of  first

00:28:05.099 --> 00:28:10.099
having  this  expert  group,  which  will
give  advice,  which  the  commission  is  then

00:28:10.099 --> 00:28:13.180
of  course  completely  free  to  ignore.
So  even  if  this  expert  group

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:18.140
comes  up  with  the  astonishing  finding
that  there  might  not  be  a  way  to  both

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:23.660
have  a  front  door  and  keep  secure
communication,  the  commission  can  still  do

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:25.279
pretty  much  whatever  they  want.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:31.240
Going  back  to  the  second  part  of  the
Protect  EU  strategy,  the  comeback  of

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:35.319
data  retention.  So  the  invalidation
of  the  data  retention  directive  in

00:28:35.319 --> 00:28:42.680
2014  left  the  commission  and  the  member
states  quite  helpless  in  an  awkward

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:46.859
situation.  They  didn't  know  what  to  do
after  that.  And  in  the  last  decade,

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:51.420
they  really  were  discussing  a
lot.  Should  we  have  another  EU

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:55.500
instrument  on  data  retention?
So  they  did  questionnaires,

00:28:55.779 --> 00:29:01.380
they  did  like  council  talks  and
whatnot.  And  what  we  could  see  from

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:07.900
leaks  by  Statewatch  notably  is  that  the
view  among  member  states  differ  a  lot.

00:29:08.099 --> 00:29:12.559
Some  member  states,  they're  really
wrong.  They're  like,  let's  not  care

00:29:12.559 --> 00:29:17.160
at  all  about  the  limits  set  by  the  court
of  justice.  Let's  go  for  what  we  want

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:21.279
and  what  we  need.  While  others  are
like,  okay,  we  can  find  a  solution  and

00:29:21.279 --> 00:29:25.819
work  within  the  limits  set  by  the  CJEU.
So  there  are  two  kind  of  camps  here.

00:29:27.039 --> 00:29:31.579
Some  member  states  are  actually  not  so
keen  to  have  new  EU  instruments  because

00:29:31.579 --> 00:29:34.140
that  would  probably  mean  that
they  would  have  to  revise

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:37.799
their  national  data  retention
regime,  which  is  illegal  by

00:29:37.799 --> 00:29:41.759
EU  law,  and  that  they  would  have
maybe  lesser  surveillance  level.

00:29:41.759 --> 00:29:44.839
So  they're  not  so  keen  to
have  a  new  EU  instruments,  but

00:29:44.839 --> 00:29:49.220
generally,  after  some  talks,
there  is  quite  a  general

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:54.640
consensus  that  there  should  be  a
new  EU  legislation  on  data  retention.

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:58.259
And  so  the  president  of  the
commission,  Ursula  von  der  Leyen,

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:02.039
announced  at  the  beginning  of  this  mandate
that  this  is  the  mandate  to  do  it.

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:05.420
They're  going  to  work  on  it.
And  so  what  we're  expecting  is

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:10.759
that  this  proposal  will  come  next
year.  The  commission  started  officially

00:30:10.759 --> 00:30:14.859
the  legislative  process  by
launching  public  consultations.

00:30:15.539 --> 00:30:21.259
And  that  really  means  that  we're  going  to
expect  a  proposal  probably  in  June  2026.

00:30:25.859 --> 00:30:32.500
What  can  we  expect  from  this  commission
proposal?  We  are  looking  at  the  summary  of

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:36.759
responses  by  member  states  to  a
questionnaire  run  by  the  Danish

00:30:36.759 --> 00:30:40.319
presidency  of  the  council.  So  they
asked,  what  are  we  going  to  do?

00:30:40.319 --> 00:30:43.920
What  are  we  asking  the  commission
to  include  in  this  proposal?

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:48.299
And  we  look  also  at  the  commission
on  call  for  evidence  or  the

00:30:48.299 --> 00:30:50.940
questions  that  they  give
to  the  public  to  answer.

00:30:51.119 --> 00:30:55.940
And  from  that,  we  can  speculate  a  little
bit.  And  what  do  we  speculate  about?

00:30:57.079 --> 00:31:02.140
The  EU  rules  will  likely  complement
national  data  retention  regimes.

00:31:02.359 --> 00:31:05.599
Again,  this  idea  of  giving
flexibility  to  member  states  to  go

00:31:05.599 --> 00:31:09.500
further  if  they  want.  If  they  want  to
surveil  more,  they  should  be  allowed  to.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:14.599
They  would  only  focus  on  certain
data  categories,  again,  leaving  the

00:31:14.599 --> 00:31:18.339
rest  to  the  member  states.
Probably  what  is  allowed  by  the

00:31:18.339 --> 00:31:25.500
CJEU,  so  identification  data,  so  to
say,  source  IP  address,  civil,  civic

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:31.380
identity  data  collected  by  the  service
providers  may  be  verified.  We  don't  know.

00:31:31.799 --> 00:31:37.920
And  probably  port  numbers,  too.  We
will  also  expect  an  enlarged  scope

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:42.319
of  service  providers  because  most
of  the  communications  are  now  taking

00:31:42.319 --> 00:31:46.740
place  elsewhere  than  the  traditional
providers  that  were  back  then

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:50.680
mainstream  and  popular  in  the  2000s.
So  they  want  to  cover  over -the -top

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:55.279
providers,  OTTs,  and  maybe
VPNs  as  well,  which  is  new.

00:31:56.519 --> 00:32:01.420
Member  states  don't  like  the  option
of  targeted  data  retention  that  is  put

00:32:01.420 --> 00:32:05.779
forward  by  the  Court  of  Justice  or  the
approach  of  quick -freeze,  preservation

00:32:05.779 --> 00:32:12.240
of  data.  They  think  it's  not  useful  for
law  enforcement,  so  probably  we  won't  see

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:17.039
that  in  the  proposal.  And  then  for
the  purpose,  what  is  allowed  to …

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:21.380
What  are  the  purpose  for
which  data  can  be  retained?

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:27.019
We  see  all  crimes  committed  in  cyberspace
or  using  information  and  communication

00:32:27.019 --> 00:32:34.299
technology.  Everything?  No?  Crimes
committed  largely  online,  stalking,

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:40.680
hate  crime,  etc.  Even  if  the  level  of
sanctions  is  moderate,  what  does  it  mean?

00:32:40.799 --> 00:32:45.140
It  means  that  it  might  go  beyond  what  the
CUEU  has  said  for  certain  categories  of

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:45.400
data.

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:48.599
Svea: Another  good  thing
to  look  at  when  we  think

00:32:48.599 --> 00:32:51.259
about  what  to  expect  is  what's
happening  at  the  member  state  level.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:57.019
So  you  might  have  seen  that  Germany  has
published  a  new  data  retention  proposal

00:32:57.019 --> 00:33:01.839
on  the  22nd  of  December,  which
was  the  Christmas  present  I  wanted.

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:06.480
And,  yeah,  it's  very  telling.  And
it's  very  similar  to  the  lines  that

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:09.240
Chloé  just  described.  So  the
German  proposal  essentially

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:15.079
proposes  the  retention  of  data  of  IP
addresses  and  port  numbers  for  up  to

00:33:15.079 --> 00:33:18.279
three  months.  This  is  a  political
compromise.  The  Conservatives  wanted  more.

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:20.660
The  SPD  had  some  backbone
and  wanted  a  bit  less.

00:33:21.819 --> 00:33:26.059
It  does  not  only  cover  internet  service
providers,  but  also  OTTs,  so  messaging

00:33:26.059 --> 00:33:29.039
services,  email  services,  and  so
on,  what  Chloé  just  mentioned.

00:33:29.859 --> 00:33:32.339
And,  yeah,  so  it's  not  great.  So  what
we're  essentially  seeing  is,  again,

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:35.500
a  proliferation.  So  this  is
a  German  attempt  to  influence

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:39.039
what  is  coming  at  the  European  level.
But  that  doesn't  mean  that  we  don't  have

00:33:39.039 --> 00:33:42.579
to  fight  it  at  the  German  level  as  well.
So  we  see  lots  of  things  happening  in

00:33:42.579 --> 00:33:45.740
Brussels,  but  unfortunately  also  things
happening  in  Germany,  but  also  in

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:51.380
Ireland,  Sweden,  and  other  places.  So  is
there  still  hope,  you  might  ask  yourself?

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:59.019
Maybe.  In  the  past,  this
community  has  looked  to

00:33:59.019 --> 00:34:02.700
courts,  and  especially  the  Court  of
Justice  of  the  European  Union,  to  fight

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.299
data  retention  and  attacks  on  encryption.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:09.159
Ever  since  the  decision  in  Digital  Rights
Ireland  in  2014  that  invalidated  the

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:15.300
European  Data  Retention  Directive,  we  looked
at  the  court  as  a  really  solid  ally.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:23.449
But  maybe  not.  Maybe  that's  no  longer  the
case,  that  the  CJEU  is  actually  an  ally.

00:34:23.869 --> 00:34:29.090
We  saw  already  in  2020,  in  the  joint
cases  brought  by  La  Quadrature  du  Net,

00:34:29.309 --> 00:34:33.610
Ligue  des  Droits  Humains  Belges,  and
Privacy  International  about  the  UK,  Belgium,

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:36.670
and  French  data  retention  regimes,  that  the

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:41.809
EU  is  starting  to  nuance
its  position  on  the

00:34:41.809 --> 00:34:47.650
general  and  indiscriminate  retention  of
traffic  and  location  data,  and  started  to

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:52.130
accept  that  it  may  be  compatible  under
certain  conditions,  or  for  certain

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:56.130
categories  of  data.  And
it's  not  going  better  since.

00:34:57.409 --> 00:35:01.710
In  2024,  the  CJEU
released  a  ruling  in  the

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:04.489
Hadopi  case,  which  is
again  a  case  brought  by

00:35:04.489 --> 00:35:07.429
La  Quadrature  du  Net,  against
the  French  anti -piracy  law.

00:35:07.949 --> 00:35:10.510
And  in  that  ruling,
the  court  accepted  that

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:15.389
mass  retention  of  source
IP  address  might  be  no

00:35:15.389 --> 00:35:18.949
longer  serious  interference  with  fundamental

00:35:18.949 --> 00:35:23.769
rights,  which  for  us
nerds  or  policy  people  is

00:35:23.769 --> 00:35:26.909
kind  of  a  departure
from  what  the  court  had

00:35:26.909 --> 00:35:30.989
previously,  from  the  approach  that
the  court  had  previously  taken.

00:35:31.630 --> 00:35:34.530
The  problem  is  that  the  CJEU  seems  to  be

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:38.989
convinced  in  these  rulings
about  the  argument  brought

00:35:38.989 --> 00:35:40.849
by  member  states  government
during  the  different

00:35:40.849 --> 00:35:44.989
hearings  on  those  cases,
which  is  new  forms  of  crimes

00:35:44.989 --> 00:35:48.949
facilitated  by  digital  means,
or  taking  place  online,

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:53.230
bring  this  risk  of  systematic  impunity.

00:35:53.929 --> 00:35:58.110
And  so  the  court  accepts
to  weaken  certain  privacy

00:35:58.110 --> 00:36:03.550
requirements  that  it  previously  held  in
order  to  prevent  this  risk  from  happening.

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:06.590
And  so  we  must  kind  of
accept  as  a  community  that

00:36:06.590 --> 00:36:10.550
litigation  is  an  important,
but  also  a  risky  tool.

00:36:11.389 --> 00:36:14.010
And  so  we  must  still
litigate  against  violation  of

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:16.309
fundamental  rights  probably,
but  it's  not  a  political

00:36:16.309 --> 00:36:18.809
strategy  to  win  this  fight  overall.

00:36:19.849 --> 00:36:22.050
Svea: It's  a  narrative
battle,  not  a  legal  one.

00:36:22.170 --> 00:36:25.230
And  I  think  this  is  actually
more  of  a  rethinking  for  this

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:26.510
community  than  we  might  be  aware  of.

00:36:26.590 --> 00:36:29.289
So  I  think  previously  a  lot
of  us  had  this  assumption  that,

00:36:29.349 --> 00:36:31.530
well,  if  they  don't  listen
to  us,  and  by  they  we  mean

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:34.210
politicians,  we'll  see  each
other  in  court  and  we'll  win

00:36:34.210 --> 00:36:36.829
because  the  jurisprudence  is
so  clear  and  there  was  this

00:36:36.829 --> 00:36:41.309
beautiful  range  and  list  of
judgments  coming  out  again  and

00:36:41.309 --> 00:36:44.349
again  making  similar  points.  But
unfortunately,  we're  not  there  anymore.

00:36:44.570 --> 00:36:47.329
So  yeah,  we  need  to  accept  that
we  need  to  move  on  and  need

00:36:47.329 --> 00:36:50.289
to  go  beyond  legal  strategy.

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:53.630
And  that  is  especially  important
because  the  other  guys  have

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:56.230
been  fighting  the  narrative
battle  for  the  past  30  years.

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:58.929
Think  back  to  the  infamous
going  dark  framing.

00:36:58.929 --> 00:37:02.070
So  what  we  have  here  is
something  really  fascinating.

00:37:02.449 --> 00:37:05.269
So  this  is  part  of  the  response
that  Slovakia  shared  to  the

00:37:05.269 --> 00:37:08.269
commission's  questionnaire  when
they  asked,  once  again,  what

00:37:08.269 --> 00:37:09.690
exactly  to  do  about  data  retention.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:13.070
And  the  Slovakian  government  thought
it  would  be  really  important

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:17.590
to  change  the  commonly  used
terminology  that  people  use  to  talk

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:18.329
about  data  retention.

00:37:18.489 --> 00:37:21.690
Because  at  this  point,  some  people
at  least  actually  understand  what

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:24.230
it's  meant  by  data  retention  and  why
it's  an  issue  for  fundamental  rights.

00:37:25.190 --> 00:37:30.289
So  Slovakia  suggested  to  change
terminology  so  that  can  eventually  lead

00:37:30.289 --> 00:37:32.389
to  a  change  in  political  perception.

00:37:32.889 --> 00:37:36.050
And  I  think  this  is  actually  a
strategy  we  should  borrow  as  a  movement.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:41.989
We  need  to  start  shifting  the  narrative
on  what  privacy  and  data  protection

00:37:41.989 --> 00:37:45.530
actually  mean  in  practice,  why  they're
relevant  for  a  lot  of  people  out  there,

00:37:45.710 --> 00:37:46.849
actually  for  every  single  one  of  them.

00:37:46.849 --> 00:37:49.989
And  we  need  to  find  new
ways  of  telling  that  story.

00:37:50.949 --> 00:37:56.610
And  if  we  do  that,  then  I  believe  that
resistance  is  definitely  possible  and  that

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:58.989
there  is  hope  left.  Yeah.

00:37:59.130 --> 00:38:03.670
So  I  think  what  we  have  seen  in  the
last  couple  of  months  in  the  country  of

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:10.909
chat  control  is  that  sometimes  we  can
win  if  we  manage  to  find  new  ways  as  a

00:38:10.909 --> 00:38:12.010
movement  to  work  on  this.

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:15.949
So  how  do  we  manage  to  shift  the  debate
on  chat  control  after  three  years  of

00:38:15.949 --> 00:38:19.150
entrenched  debates  and  really  bad
proposals  from  absolutely  every  direction?

00:38:19.389 --> 00:38:23.869
What  can  we  learn  from  that  fight  to  look
at  these  future  fights  that  are,  again,

00:38:23.949 --> 00:38:27.190
the  exact  same  proposals  as  the  past  30
years  but  just  in  a  slightly  different

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:27.969
political  context?

00:38:28.429 --> 00:38:31.969
One  thing  we  have  learned  is  that  we  need
to  build  broad  coalitions  with  unlikely

00:38:31.969 --> 00:38:34.050
partners.  This  is  really  crucial.

00:38:34.309 --> 00:38:38.130
We  need  to  work  with  people  who  we  might
not  agree  with  on  every  single  issue  but

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:40.190
that  we  agree  with  on  the  big  questions.

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:44.329
So  in  the  case  of  chat  control,  some
of  our  most  important  allies  are  child

00:38:44.329 --> 00:38:45.190
protection  organizations.

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:49.150
And  these  are  people  who  have  a  lot  of
expertise  on  issues  that  we  don't  have

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:49.750
expertise  on.

00:38:49.889 --> 00:38:53.889
And  they  can  talk  with  a  lot  of  legitimacy
about  what  it  actually  takes  to  protect

00:38:53.889 --> 00:38:57.309
children  from  abuse  that  has
nothing  to  do  with  chat  control.

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:01.429
So  these  are  the  kinds  of  alliances
that  might  be  unfamiliar  and  a  bit

00:39:01.429 --> 00:39:06.050
uncomfortable  at  first  that  we  need  to
build  now  in  order  to  fight  back  against

00:39:06.050 --> 00:39:06.590
this  effectively.

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:10.750
The  other  big  thing  is,  and  this  is  something
I  kind  of  briefly  mentioned  before,  is  that

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:13.969
we  need  to  invite  more  people
to  understand  why  this  matter.

00:39:14.630 --> 00:39:18.809
So  in  this  talk,  Chloé  and  I  essentially  only
talked  about  the  past  and  then  tried  to  give

00:39:18.809 --> 00:39:23.170
you  an  idea  of  what's  coming,  but  we  really
didn't  talk  a  lot  about  why  this  is  bad,  right?

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:27.989
We  kind  of  operated  from  the  assumption  that
everyone  in  this  room  already  agrees  with  us  on

00:39:27.989 --> 00:39:31.610
the  fact  that  mass  surveillance  both
from  states  and  companies  is  bad.

